From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 22:31:25 +0200
Subject: net: lantiq: gswip: describe the sub-apertures as reg-less children

The device tree described one register block as three devices. gswl:
gswip@1c000000 claims <0x1c000000 0x4000> and its driver takes that
window with devm_platform_ioremap_resource(), which does
request_mem_region(); the GPHY firmware loader and the MDIO bus then sat
at root scope with reg ranges *inside* it, 0x1c003c00 + 0x280 and
0x1c003c10 + 0x20, the second also inside the first. Their drivers used
bare devm_ioremap() precisely to dodge the -EBUSY a resource claim would
have produced, and one of them said so in a comment. GSW-R had the same
shape at 0x1a003c10, plus an MDIO controller at root scope with no bus
above it.

Mainline already models this exact problem for the previous generation
of the same switch and solves it the other way:
Documentation/devicetree/bindings/net/dsa/lantiq,gswip.yaml puts mdio
and gphy-fw as children of the switch node with no reg of their own, and
drivers/net/dsa/lantiq/lantiq_gswip.c finds them by name. The vendor
device tree for this SoC gives its MDIO node no reg either. So the two
nodes become reg-less children named gphy-fw and mdio, and each driver
derives its window from the parent switch node's reg plus its offset
into the IP block -- 0x3c00 for the base-address registers, 0x3c10 for
the bus. Those offsets are block-relative, so they are identical on both
instances and the GSW-R bus needs nothing the GSW-L one does not have.
One reg claim remains, and it is the switch's.

The switch probe creates the children with devm_of_platform_populate()
rather than registering the bus and loading the firmware itself the way
lantiq_gswip.c does, because the two halves cannot share a module here:
the GPHY firmware is proprietary and comes from the rootfs, so the
loader and the bus build as intel-xrx500-mdio.ko while the switch core
is built in. Folding their work into the switch probe would put
request_firmware() in an initcall, long before there is a filesystem to
read it from. Keeping them as platform devices with their own drivers
also keeps the deferred probe that waits for the firmware, and
populating with a NULL match table stops the recursion at the children,
so the ethernet-phy@N grandchildren stay MDIO devices. Each child
carries the lantiq,gswip-instance value of its parent, which is what
selects the GSW-L switch-core re-init.

The DT half has to move in the same commit. The blob ships with the
kernel image, and neither half degrades: reg-less nodes under the old
drivers fail probe for want of a resource, and the new drivers under the
old device tree look for a parent reg that the root-scope nodes do not
have. Either way the board comes up with no MDIO bus and no GPHY
firmware, which is a board with no network.

Comments that only described the old arrangement go with it: the
paragraph arguing that the overlapping root-scope reg claims were by
design, and the one naming both a reg value and a file
(seale_avm_fritz7560.dtsi) that no longer exist. The claim that the
firmware has to be built in goes too -- it is loaded from the rootfs,
which is why its driver is a module.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm.dtsi b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm.dtsi
index 4f99e42af75b..3fd7be6ff80b 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm.dtsi
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm.dtsi
@@ -36,42 +36,38 @@ nand0: nand@0 {
 	};
 };
 
-/* Both GSWIP-3.0 instances; on-die blocks with no board wiring. */
+/*
+ * Both GSWIP-3.0 instances; on-die blocks with no board wiring.
+ *
+ * The GPHY firmware loader and the MDIO bus are not devices of their own:
+ * their registers are sub-apertures of the switch register window this node
+ * already claims. They are therefore reg-less children, and each driver
+ * derives its window from this node's reg plus a fixed offset into the IP
+ * block. The switch driver creates the platform devices for them.
+ */
 &gswl {
 	status = "okay";
-};
-
-&gswr {
-	status = "okay";
-};
 
-/ {
 	/*
-	 * GPHY firmware loader. The 0x280 window must cover the per-GPHY
-	 * LBADR/MBADR pairs at offsets 0x228..0x26f. The firmware must be
-	 * built in: this driver probes long before any rootfs is mounted.
+	 * GPHY firmware loader, at switch base + 0x3c00. Its window must
+	 * cover the per-GPHY LBADR/MBADR pairs at 0x228..0x26f within it.
 	 */
-	gphy_fw: phy-xrx500@1c003c00 {
+	gphy_fw: gphy-fw {
 		compatible = "lantiq,phy-xrx500";
-		reg = <0x1c003c00 0x280>;
 		firmware-name = "lantiq/xrx500-phy-fw.bin";
 		status = "okay";
 	};
 
 	/*
-	 * GSWIP-3.0 MDIO bus on the GSW-L instance. The aperture is the
-	 * switch base plus (MDCTRL dword 0x004 + GSW30_TOP_OFFSET 0xf00) * 4,
-	 * a 0x20 span covering MDCTRL, MDREAD, MDWRITE and the two MDCCFG
-	 * registers. It overlaps the switch node's own reg claim on purpose,
-	 * which is why the driver maps it with devm_ioremap() rather than
-	 * requesting the region.
+	 * MDIO bus, at switch base + (MDCTRL dword 0x004 + GSW30_TOP_OFFSET
+	 * 0xf00) * 4 = 0x3c10, a 0x20 span covering MDCTRL, MDREAD, MDWRITE
+	 * and the two MDCCFG registers.
 	 *
 	 * The bus defers probing until the GPHY firmware loader has run: a
 	 * PHY without firmware never answers MDIO.
 	 */
-	gswip_mdio: gswip-mdio@1c003c10 {
+	gswip_mdio: mdio {
 		compatible = "lantiq,gswip-mdio";
-		reg = <0x1c003c10 0x20>;
 		lantiq,gswip-instance = <0>;
 		#address-cells = <1>;
 		#size-cells = <0>;
@@ -112,7 +108,13 @@ gphy_lan4: ethernet-phy@2 {
 			reg = <0x02>;
 		};
 	};
+};
 
+&gswr {
+	status = "okay";
+};
+
+/ {
 	/*
 	 * Datapath netdev parent. Each child names one front-panel port and
 	 * its datapath port id; the driver allocates one netdev per child.
diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_grx550.dtsi b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_grx550.dtsi
index a2ec4dac78a5..10b3fa80aa9c 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_grx550.dtsi
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_grx550.dtsi
@@ -28,33 +28,6 @@ usb_vbus: regulator-usb-vbus {
 		enable-active-high;
 	};
 
-	/*
-	 * The second MDIO bus, on the GSW-R instance, carrying the WAN PHY.
-	 * The aperture is the GSW-L arithmetic in seale_avm.dtsi applied to
-	 * the GSW-R base. The vendor device tree gives its equivalent node no
-	 * reg, hanging the bus off the switch node instead, so there is no
-	 * vendor counterpart to check this address against; if the arithmetic
-	 * is wrong the symptom is specific, reads return 0xffff and the WAN
-	 * PHY never enumerates.
-	 *
-	 * lantiq,gswip-instance = <1> keeps the GSW-L switch-core re-init off
-	 * this bus. The PHY is at MDIO address 1.
-	 */
-	gswr_mdio: gswip-mdio@1a003c10 {
-		compatible = "lantiq,gswip-mdio";
-		reg = <0x1a003c10 0x20>;
-		lantiq,gswip-instance = <1>;
-		#address-cells = <1>;
-		#size-cells = <0>;
-		status = "okay";
-
-		gphy_wan: ethernet-phy@1 {
-			compatible = "ethernet-phy-idd565.a409",
-				     "ethernet-phy-ieee802.3-c22";
-			reg = <0x01>;
-		};
-	};
-
 	/*
 	 * The blink generator's slow clock source: a fixed 8 kHz input the
 	 * driver divides for its blink table.
@@ -98,6 +71,31 @@ ssogpio: ssogpio@16d00000 {
 	};
 };
 
+/*
+ * The second MDIO bus, on the GSW-R instance, carrying the WAN PHY. Same
+ * sub-aperture of the same IP block as the GSW-L bus in seale_avm.dtsi, so
+ * the driver reaches it through the identical offset from this switch node's
+ * own base.
+ *
+ * lantiq,gswip-instance = <1> keeps the GSW-L switch-core re-init off this
+ * bus. The PHY is at MDIO address 1.
+ */
+&gswr {
+	gswr_mdio: mdio {
+		compatible = "lantiq,gswip-mdio";
+		lantiq,gswip-instance = <1>;
+		#address-cells = <1>;
+		#size-cells = <0>;
+		status = "okay";
+
+		gphy_wan: ethernet-phy@1 {
+			compatible = "ethernet-phy-idd565.a409",
+				     "ethernet-phy-ieee802.3-c22";
+			reg = <0x01>;
+		};
+	};
+};
+
 /*
  * All five GPHYs. The reset bit list is not contiguous, so every entry is
  * spelled out; on this die bit 31 is the fifth PHY's own reset line and not a
diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/switch-api/gsw_init.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/switch-api/gsw_init.c
index 007be2459beb..db4485246790 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/switch-api/gsw_init.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/lantiq/switch-api/gsw_init.c
@@ -19,6 +19,7 @@
 #include <linux/mod_devicetable.h>
 #include <linux/module.h>
 #include <linux/of.h>
+#include <linux/of_platform.h>
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
 #include <linux/spinlock.h>
 #include <linux/types.h>
@@ -158,6 +159,21 @@ static int intel_xrx500_gswip_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 				"gsw_init: dp_init_module failed=%d\n", dp_ret);
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * The GPHY firmware loader and the MDIO bus are sub-apertures of the
+	 * window mapped above, described as reg-less children of this node, so
+	 * nothing else instantiates them. Their drivers live in a module of
+	 * their own - the GPHY firmware comes from the rootfs, so they must not
+	 * be pulled into this initcall - and bind to these devices when that
+	 * module loads.
+	 */
+	ret = devm_of_platform_populate(dev);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_err(dev, "failed to create switch sub-nodes: %d\n", ret);
+		gsw_devs_unregister(devid);
+		return ret;
+	}
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
